{
  "id": "asoxSchedAlrtQue",
  "name": "sched-alert-queue",
  "active": true,
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## sched-alert-queue (alert queue scheduler, N8N-1.4, N8N-1.7)\n**Trigger:** every 5 s.\n**Inputs:** the Data Tables `asox_alert_queue` and `asox_queue_slots`; lib-config `alert_queue` (only read when the queue has rows).\n**Actions taken:** with nothing pending or running, stops at once (and the run is not saved). Otherwise: requeues `running` rows older than `running_timeout_s` (or fails and parks them after `max_attempts`), deletes `done` rows older than `keep_done_h`, takes a free slot (compare-and-set on the slot's `claim`, read back), then **loops**: claims the oldest pending row (FIFO by row id; compare-and-set `pending` -> `running` with a new token, read back), runs **lib-alert-queue-drain** for it and waits, and goes on with the next row until no pending row is left, `max_rows_per_run` rows were drained or `max_run_s` has passed (checked before each claim). Before each claim the slot's `held_ms` is refreshed (compare-and-set on our token, read back), so a held slot is never older than its current row; a lost slot ends the run. A row this run couldn't finish (claim lost, given back, failed attempt) is skipped until the next run. Then the slot is freed. At most `alert_queue.slots` rows run at once, because each run holds one slot; with every slot held a tick does nothing, and a freed slot is taken by the next tick.\n**Systems changed:** n8n's own Data Tables (the playbook changes TheHive).\n**Approval:** not needed: n8n's own Data Tables only; each playbook declares its own approval.\n**Owner:** n8n.\nSaved: failed runs only. The work is recorded in lib-alert-queue-drain's executions (saved, one per row), so the 17,000 idle ticks a day keep nothing. The loop keeps only row ids, outcomes and durations (`Record row`).",
        "height": 760,
        "width": 560
      },
